# Response headers, served by the origin.
#
# These four used to be a Cloudflare "Security headers" Transform Rule, because
# GitHub Pages cannot set a header at all and the edge was the only place they
# could live. Cloudflare Pages can, so they move into the repository, where they
# are diffed and reviewed like everything else rather than living in a dashboard
# nobody opens.
#
# **Every value below is byte-identical to what the Transform Rule sends today**,
# read back off the live response rather than retyped from the dashboard. That is
# deliberate and it is the whole migration plan: while both exist the browser
# receives two identical policies and enforces the same thing twice, so deleting
# the rule is provably a no-op rather than a change nobody watched. Change a value
# here only after the rule is gone.
#
# Two headers are missing from this file on purpose. `Strict-Transport-Security`
# and `X-Content-Type-Options` come from the HSTS dialog under SSL/TLS, not from
# the Transform Rule, and setting them here as well would send each one twice.
# They stay where they are.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' https://sleepercdn.com https://*.giphy.com; connect-src 'self' https://juke-draft-room.jukeff.workers.dev wss://juke-draft-room.jukeff.workers.dev https://site.api.espn.com; manifest-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'
